
// The heavy.loc and jobs.loc examples were built and run on 2026-10-13, in
// process and with `morloc make --slurm` against a mock sbatch/sacct that
// runs each job on the same machine. No real cluster was used.

[[remote-jobs]]
=== Remote job submission

A labeled call can run as a separate job on a SLURM cluster. This is for the
step that needs more than the machine running the program: 32 GB and eight
cores, called once per sample across hundreds of samples. You describe the
job's resources in the label's configuration, and each call through the label
is submitted with `sbatch`, waited for, and its result returned to the program
as if it had run locally.

[WARNING]
.Experimental Feature
====

Remote jobs are tested against a stand-in for `sbatch` and `sacct` that runs
each job on the same machine, not on a real cluster. Setting up a cluster for
them is covered in <<execution-contexts>>.

====

==== Sending a call to a job

A label (see <<logging>>) with a `remote:` block makes every call through it a
job:

[source, morloc]
.heavy.loc
----
module heavy (foo)

import root-py
source Py from "heavy.py" ("run", "reduce")

run :: Str -> Int
reduce :: [Int] -> Int

foo :: [Str] -> Int
foo = reduce . map big@run
----

[source, python]
.heavy.py
----
def run(x):
    return len(x)

def reduce(xs):
    return sum(xs)
----

[source, yaml]
.heavy.yaml
----
labeled-groups:
  big:
    remote:
      threads: 8
      memory: 32
      time: "01:00:00"
----

Here `run` is mapped over three inputs, so one run of `foo` submits three
jobs, one per call of `run`. `reduce` runs in the program itself.

Job submission is compiled in only when the build asks for it. Without it, a
labeled call runs in the program as usual, so the same program works on a
laptop:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o heavy heavy.loc
$ ./heavy foo '["a","bcd","ef"]'
6
$ morloc make --slurm -o heavy heavy.loc
$ ./heavy foo '["a","bcd","ef"]'
6
----

The second build submits jobs. `morloc make --slurm` turns submission on for
one build; `morloc init --slurm` turns it on for every build in the
environment.

==== Describing the job

The `remote:` block lists the resources each job asks for. Every field is
optional; a field you leave out is not requested, and the cluster's default
applies. A `memory` or `time` of 0 is passed on, and SLURM reads it as all of
a node's memory or no time limit; `threads` or `gpus` of 0 is not requested.

[cols="1,2,2", options="header"]
|===
| Field | Meaning | Requested as
| `threads` | CPU cores | `--cpus-per-task=N`
| `memory` | memory in gigabytes | `--mem=NG`
| `time` | wall time, `"HH:MM:SS"` or `"D-HH:MM:SS"` | `--time=D-HH:MM:SS`
| `gpus` | GPUs | `--gres=gpu:N`
|===

`time` must be a string; a bare number of seconds is refused when the program
is built:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o heavy heavy.loc
Failed to parse module config file 'heavy.loc': Aeson exception:
Error in $['labeled-groups'].big.remote.time: Expected a string for SLURM time
----

==== What runs in the job

A label wraps exactly one call of the function it is written on. The job runs
that function and everything the function's own body calls, including its
recursive calls. Everything else stays in the program that made the call, which
the rest of this section calls the job's *parent*.

The arguments of a labeled call are computed by the parent before the job is
submitted, and the job receives them as values. In `big@run (prepare x)`,
`prepare x` runs in the parent and `run` runs in the job. To send `prepare` to
the job as well, name the whole computation and label the name. With a sourced
`prepare :: Str -> Str` added to `heavy.loc`:

[source, morloc]
----
runPrepared :: Str -> Int
runPrepared x = run (prepare x)

foo :: [Str] -> Int
foo = reduce . map big@runPrepared
----

A label goes on a name, never on a larger expression. Writing
`foo xs = big@(reduce xs)` is refused:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc make -o heavy heavy.loc
heavy.loc:10:10: A label goes on the name of a function or a suspension, never on a larger expression; give the expression a name and label that ('big@f x', where 'f' computes it)
     |
  10 | foo xs = big@(reduce xs)
     |          ^
----

A job can submit jobs of its own. If `runPrepared` called `big@run`, each
`runPrepared` job would be the parent of a `run` job. Removing the inner label
runs `run` inside the outer job instead.

==== Effects

A job is where expensive work happens, and expensive work usually reads files
or queries a database: it has an effect (see <<effects>>). A labeled call to an
effectful function runs the effect in the job. A label on a suspension makes
each run of the suspension a job:

[source, morloc]
.jobs.loc
----
module jobs (summarize)

import root-py
source Py from "jobs.py" ("load", "total")

load :: Str -> <IO> [Int]
total :: [Int] -> Int

dataset :: <IO> [Int]
dataset = load "counts.txt"

summarize :: Int -> <IO> Int
summarize x = do
  xs <- big@dataset
  total xs
----

[source, python]
.jobs.py
----
def load(path):
    with open(path) as fh:
        return [int(line) for line in fh]

def total(xs):
    return sum(xs)
----

Running `big@dataset` submits a job that reads `counts.txt` and returns its
numbers; `total` then runs in the parent. A suspension that is run twice is two
jobs. When the program calls `sbatch` itself, a job starts in the directory
the program was started from, so the relative path `counts.txt` names the same
file there.

[source, console]
----
$ printf '3\n4\n5\n' > counts.txt
$ morloc make --slurm -o jobs jobs.loc
$ ./jobs summarize 1
12
----

==== Results

Each call submits a new job. A job's inputs and result pass through files in a
directory the parent and the job share (<<eco-remote>>), and the parent
deletes them once it has read the result. To keep a job's result and skip the
job the next time the same call is made, ask for the cache as well:

[source, yaml]
----
labeled-groups:
  big:
    cache: true
    remote:
      threads: 8
      memory: 32
      time: "01:00:00"
----

The cache is checked before the job is submitted, so a hit submits nothing;
<<caching>> describes when a call counts as the same call.

A job that fails is reported with its final state and the start of its standard
error, and the program stops. Here the job cannot find its input:

[source, console]
----
$ rm counts.txt
$ ./jobs summarize 1
Error: run failed
Error (pymorloc.c:3235 in pybinding__remote_call):
SLURM job 104 ended FAILED; its stderr is .../cache/_remote/.../job.err:
Error: run failed: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'counts.txt'
...
----

A failed job's files are kept, so you can read the whole of its standard error
at the path the message gives.

==== What a job can receive

An argument or result crosses to another machine, so it must be a value that
means the same thing there. A file handle (`IFile`) is sent as its path, which
the job opens on the shared filesystem. An input or output stream (`IStream`,
`OStream`) belongs to the running program and cannot be sent to a job; a call
that tries is an error. A job also cannot return an `IStream`.

